---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import screen from '../../assets/conomi-resumen.webp';
import services from '../../assets/conomi-servicios.webp';
import { portfolioProject as project, mailto } from '../../data/cv';
---
<Base title="Conomi · A shipped mobile product by Daniel Mora" description="A closer look at Conomi: product design, React Native and Expo engineering, local behavior and release to iOS and Android.">
  <article class="case-study wrap">
    <a class="case-back" href="/">← Back to portfolio</a>
    <header class="case-header"><p class="work-context">Independent product · iOS & Android</p><h1>Conomi<span>.</span></h1><p>A finance app shaped around Mexican credit cards, payment dates and installment purchases.</p><div class="store-evidence"><a href={project.links.website}>Live website ↗</a><a href={project.links.apple}>App Store ↗</a><a href={project.links.google}>Google Play ↗</a></div></header>
    <div class="case-intro"><div><dl class="case-scope"><div><dt>My role</dt><dd>Creator · product design & engineering</dd></div><div><dt>Stack</dt><dd>TypeScript · React Native · Expo</dd></div><div><dt>Delivery</dt><dd>Published on App Store and Google Play</dd></div><div><dt>Source</dt><dd>Private repository · publicly released app</dd></div></dl><section><h2>The problem</h2><p>With several credit cards and installment purchases, “what do I owe this month?” becomes a calculation across different statement dates, plans and recurring payments. Conomi organizes those commitments without asking for banking credentials.</p></section><section><h2>What I built</h2><p>I designed the product experience, implemented the React Native app and shipped it to iOS and Android. The work spans schedules and payment projections, local reminders, exports and backups, and release updates.</p></section></div><figure class="case-device"><img src={screen.src} width={screen.width} height={screen.height} alt="Full Conomi debt summary screen, with sample credit balances, payment dates and a monthly debt projection." loading="eager" /><figcaption>Actual released app screen · sample data</figcaption></figure></div>
    <section class="case-decisions" aria-labelledby="decisions-title"><h2 id="decisions-title">Engineering decisions and their cost</h2><ol><li><h3>The data stays on the phone.</h3><p>Cards, purchases and installments live in a SQLite database on the device. An account is optional and the app works fully without one. The cost: no automatic cloud copy, so changing phones depends on a backup file.</p></li><li><h3>Imports wait for review.</h3><p>A spreadsheet never turns straight into purchases. A review screen flags likely duplicates, unreadable rows and unknown cards, and asks for one decision per card instead of one per row. Every import takes one more step, and it can be undone afterwards.</p></li><li><h3>Reminders that fit the platform.</h3><p>Reminders are local notifications. iOS drops anything past 64 pending, so the app schedules at most 56 within a three month window and refreshes them daily. If someone stops opening the app, coverage ends with a last reminder to open it.</p></li></ol><dl class="case-scope"><div><dt>Testing</dt><dd>Date and money logic runs under Jest pinned to Mexico City time, plus integration suites on a real SQLite engine that check SQL and JavaScript agree on statement cycles.</dd></div><div><dt>Release</dt><dd>A manual workflow gates on lint, types and tests, builds with EAS and submits to both stores. Seven production versions since July 2026.</dd></div></dl></section>
    <section class="case-services"><div><p class="work-context">More than a transaction list</p><h2>Recurring commitments,<br />in the same picture.</h2><p>The app also tracks services and subscriptions with their frequencies and payment setup, so a monthly view reflects more than purchases alone.</p></div><figure><img src={services.src} width={services.width} height={services.height} alt="Conomi recurring services screen showing monthly commitments and sample subscription amounts." loading="lazy" /><figcaption>Actual app screen · sample data</figcaption></figure></section>
    <section class="case-outcome"><h2>The outcome is inspectable.</h2><p>Conomi is released on both stores. You can inspect the app, its public feature descriptions and release notes. The source repository stays private.</p><div class="store-evidence"><a href={project.links.apple}>Inspect the iOS release ↗</a><a href={project.links.google}>Inspect the Android release ↗</a></div></section>
    <section class="case-contact"><h2>Need someone who can take a product through release?</h2><a class="button" href={mailto}>Email me about a role</a><a href="/resume/">View résumé →</a></section>
  </article>
</Base>
